  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms\nare expected today.\n\nAs of 330 AM, radar imagery shows numerous showers and\nthunderstorms ongoing across much of southern Ohio and northern\nWest Virginia. This activity will continue to track eastward\ntoward our area over the next several hours, but is expected to\nweaken as it moves east of the Appalachians into an environment\ncharacterized by lesser instability. However, it's likely that \nat least some showers and maybe an embedded thunderstorms or two\nwill track eastward across the area this morning. This activity\nis expected to eventually dissipate entirely, but it does cast \nsome uncertainty regarding whether additional thunderstorm \nactivity will be able to form in its wake this afternoon.\n\nOn the synoptic scale, a warm front will be lifting northward\nthrough the forecast area today, with higher dewpoint air \nstreaming in from the south and west. Most guidance does show at\nleast some destabilization today as the deeper moisture works \nin and limited surface heating occurs through a broken cloud \ndeck. Temperatures are forecast to climb into the low to mid 80s\nthis afternoon, with dewpoints reaching into the lower 70s. \nThis yields around 500-1000 J/kg of MLCAPE. Model guidance also \nshows a weak shortwave disturbance moving through around peak \nheating. The best forcing for ascent associated with this \nfeature and the greatest instability are both forecast to be \nacross the southern half of the forecast area. While an \nafternoon thunderstorm can't be ruled out anywhere, the greatest\nchance for a storm appears to be roughly south of I-66 and east\nof the Blue Ridge. However, forecast uncertainty remains high \nwith the activity from WV/OH moving through this morning, which \nisn't being handled particularly well by model guidance. Even if\nstorms do form, the background environment doesn't look \nfavorable for severe storms.\n\nA cold front will move through the area tonight, causing winds\nto shift around to out of the north. Low clouds will build in \novernight, with low temperatures forecast to be in the upper 50s\nto mid 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Cool and wet conditions continue through the\nend of the week.\n\nContinuing northeasterly flow, strong high pressure to our\nnorth, and a developing low pressure system trailing up the \neastern seaboard sets up a classic cold air damming environment \nacross the Mid- Atlantic through much of the week. Temperatures\nremain well- below late-September averages due to persistent \ncloud cover and a stable cool airmass remaining trapped against \nthe Blue Ridge. Several short- wave disturbances tracking across\nthe Mid- Atlantic sustain on and off showers through the end of\nthe week. Substantial cloud cover Thursday and Friday reduce \ninstability and thunderstorm potential. \n\nThis coastal low pressure system offshore continues to move \nnorthward coinciding with a strengthening high pressure system \nlingering in Quebec. Tightening pressure gradients over our \nregion particularly across our Coastal Plains, could lead to \nbreezier winds late Thursday and Friday. Continued onshore flow \ncombined with stronger tidal influence could increase coastal \nflood risk during this period. Refer to the coastal flooding \nsection below for more details. \n\nAs this system tracks to the north of our region by the weekend, \nwindier conditions fade. Shifting northwesterly wind ushers in drier \nconditions and decreasing shower potential to start of the\nweekend.",
